NNAAMMEE
     nnooss--ttuunn — implement ``nos'' or ``ka9q'' style IP over IP tunnel

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     nnooss--ttuunn --tt _t_u_n_n_e_l --ss _s_o_u_r_c_e --dd _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n --pp _p_r_o_t_o_c_o_l___n_u_m_b_e_r [_s_o_u_r_c_e]
             _t_a_r_g_e_t

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The nnooss--ttuunn utility is used to establish an _n_o_s style tunnel, (also known
     as _k_a_9_q or _I_P_-_I_P tunnel) using a tun(4) kernel interface.

     _T_u_n_n_e_l is the name of the tunnel device _/_d_e_v_/_t_u_n_0 for example.

     _S_o_u_r_c_e and _d_e_s_t_i_n_a_t_i_o_n are the addresses used on the tunnel device.  If
     you configure the tunnel against a cisco router, use a netmask of
     “255.255.255.252” on the cisco.  This is because the tunnel is a point-
     to-point interface in the FreeBSD end, a concept cisco does not really
     implement.

     _P_r_o_t_o_c_o_l _n_u_m_b_e_r sets tunnel mode.  Original KA9Q NOS uses 94 but many
     people use 4 on the worldwide backbone of ampr.org.

     _T_a_r_g_e_t is the address of the remote tunnel device, this must match the
     source address set on the remote end.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     This end, a FreeBSD box on address 192.168.59.34:

           nos-tun -t /dev/tun0 -s 192.168.61.1 -d 192.168.61.2 192.168.56.45

     Remote cisco on address 192.168.56.45:

           interface tunnel 0
           ip address 192.168.61.2 255.255.255.252
           tunnel mode nos
           tunnel destination 192.168.59.34
           tunnel source 192.168.56.45

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The nnooss--ttuunn utility appeared in FreeBSD 3.0.

